{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Gossa73eRLihYqPzjJMYre","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Wait, looking at the task requirements more carefully:\n\nThe task title says \"BuildBinary helper (srd001-testutils R4.1-R4.4)\" but the SRD's R4.1-R4.4 are about normalization hooks, not BuildBinary. BuildBinary isn't even in the SRD requirements - the SRD non_goals say \"pkg/testutils does not build or compile the Go binaries under test.\"\n\nBut looking at the task requirements section:\n- R1: Implement BuildBinary\n- R2: Use t.TempDir()\n\n\n- R3: t.Fatal on failure\n- R4: Binary name from directory name\n\nAnd looking at the existing code, BuildBinary already exists (lines 277-290). The task says \"Replace BuildBinary stub with full implementation\". Let me check if the current implementation satisfies all requirements. The existing code sets up a temp directory, runs go build with stderr capture, and calls t.Fatalf on error...\n\nFinishing the error handling...\n\nNow comparing against the requirements: R1 and R2 are covered, R3 is handled with t.Fatal, but R4 isn't met yet since the binary name is hardcoded as \"binary\" instead of being derived from the directory name. According to the design decision, I need to use filepath.Base(dir) to extract the basename, though I'll need to handle the edge case where dir is \".\" by resolving it to the actual directory name first. D3: \"BuildBinary must work when called from within the Go module. It compiles cmd/ packages using relative paths like '.'\"\n\nSo when dir is \".\" filepath.Base returns \".\" - that's not a great binary name. I should handle this case. Let me think...\n\nWhen dir is \".\", the package directory is the current working directory. I can use `os.Getwd()` to get the absolute path and then `filepath.Base()` on that. Or I could just use a fixed fallback name.\n\nActually, rethinking - the design decision D2 says \"basename is the